@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix foundation: <http://foundation.local/ontology/> .

# =============================================================================
# APIKey
# =============================================================================
# A unique identifier used to authenticate requests to an API service
#
# Version: 0.1.0
# License: GNU GPL
# =============================================================================

foundation:APIKey a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf foundation:Credential ;
    rdfs:label "API Key" ;
    rdfs:comment "A unique identifier used to authenticate requests to an API service" ;
    foundation:icon "vpn_key" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso """
Examples:
- Claude AI API key (sk-ant-...)
- OpenAI API key
- Google Maps API key
- GitHub Personal Access Token
- Stripe API key

API keys are typically:
- Long alphanumeric strings
- Used in HTTP headers or query parameters
- Service-specific (non-transferable between services)
- Can be revoked by the service provider
- Should be kept secret and not committed to version control
""" .
